A||||9||jlruiz67||best tire for stock rims?||||5||
Z||000000||jlruiz67||10-29-2000||01:42 PM||||Hello all <BR>I like to know what is the best price vs performance vs size for stock 15" rims on my 87 GT front and rear. I think I'm one of the few that acturally likes the stock rims on my GT. I was thinking on going 215-50-15 on front and 225-50-15 rear with a lowering kit,I like to know what would be a good brand and model and if anyone knows how big can I go on the rear. Thank you much.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||10-29-2000||02:17 PM||||The Dunlop D60A2 tires are excellent tires. They were rated number one by Consumer Reports in the past and are also inexpensive. They outperformed tirs costing twice as much. I have used them on my Impala and they made a very noticeable difference in the way the car handled and responded to steering inputs. They cost around $60-$70 each.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||10-29-2000||02:26 PM||||I replied to the same question under General Chat.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||10-29-2000||03:32 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jlruiz67:<BR><B>I think I'm one of the few that acturally likes the stock rims on my GT.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LOL.. true, true.. I don't really like the GT "lace" wheels.. they don't look modern enough to match the Fiero styling IMHO. I prefer the tech wheels, on all Fiero styles.. unless you're going to get aftermarket ones!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bluegt.jpg">   <BR>(p.s. no this isn't my car. I found this photo on the net.)<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 10-29-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000004||Tom88gt||10-29-2000||05:21 PM||||My vote is for the Dunlop D60 A2s as well.  Put them on my 300ZX, 914 and when the Fiero needs new shoes...Dunlop D60 A2s again.  Why?  Great all weather performance tire with a great price.  Consumer Reports picked a winner!||||reg||1
Z||000005||jlruiz67||10-29-2000||05:39 PM||||You guy's are great, now how about size, I'm having problems finding the Dunlop D60 A2s on the size that I was looking for "I was thinking on going 215-50-15 on front and 225-50-15 rear""if anyone knows how big can I go on the rear"<P>from Oreif<BR>"I have a set of BFGoodrich 225/50R15 Euro T/A's on a set of GT wheels. These are mounted on my lowered 1986 SE. I have no rubbing problems and it handles like it's glued to the road!"<BR>How about steering with that big ruber up front?<P>Thank you much<p>[This message has been edited by jlruiz67 (edited 10-29-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000006||Oreif||10-29-2000||09:02 PM||||Steering is easier since I went to the poly bushings and lowered it. It feels like the car turns easier than it did in stock form. I think the turning easier is the bushings and the lowering kit due to the aspect of the suspension. As for the rear you can technically go to a 235/50R15 but I wouldn't go over 225. If too much of the tire goes outside of the rim support handling will be affected. I prefer function over appearence when it comes to handling.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Greg Piet||10-29-2000||11:03 PM||||BFG G-Force T/A R1<BR>205/60/15<P>they are twice the price of the Dunlops mentioned above but I am certain the Dunlops wont come close to performance...  they may last 5 times as long though!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>They are DOT legal tires, but designed for autocross racing.  In all reality I dont know how feasable they are for daily driver usage, but they will definately stick.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wannabe||10-30-2000||07:56 AM||||Forget about the R1's for the street. They are simply not suitable...two times around the block and they are pure racing slick. For really good value, check out tires from Sumitomo (HTR 100) Hankook and Kumho. All very good tires available at excellent prices. Also, 225-50-15 works well all around. I currently have 225-55-15 Michelin Pilots all around, but these tires are very hard to find and very expensive.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||04-16-2001||09:33 AM||||So the 225/50 is a good size for performance, etc.  Will that fit on the front 88 rims, since they're only 6" wide?  ||||reg||1
